# =====================================================================================================================================================
# frontend/admin/.env.example   -   the ADMIN APPLICATION (the staff site: admin.rootsquares.com; Vite, port 5275 locally)
# Written for W26-R52 (2026-11-03). This application reads exactly ONE name (a test, frontend/tests/hardening.test.mjs, keeps it so). Full reference: docs/setup/13-environment-variables.md.
# =====================================================================================================================================================
#
# EVERY VITE_ VALUE IS PUBLIC (written into the page at build time). Never put a secret here. A changed value reaches the site only after a NEW BUILD:
# restart `npm run dev:admin`, or redeploy the static site on Render.
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Make the file for your environment by copying this one (all three are git-ignored, none is ever committed). This folder had no env file before; locally the default below works
#      with the single local API on port 5001, so .env.local is only needed when you run the two services apart.
#        .env.local        your machine.
#        .env.staging      a notebook for the Render STAGING static site (guide 41 Step 5), and the file `--mode staging` uses on this machine.
#        .env.production   the same for production.
#      Render never sees these files. On Render you paste the line into the static site's Environment tab.
#   2. Build or run on this machine with another file (PowerShell, from frontend\):
#        npm run dev -w @rootsquares/admin -- --mode staging
#        npm run build -w @rootsquares/admin -- --mode staging
#      (Use these forms. The shortcut `npm run dev:admin -- --mode staging` does NOT pass the mode on to Vite.) Vite reads .env, .env.local, .env.staging (the later wins). A value already in the shell or in Render's dashboard always wins over every file.
#
# WHERE:  L = .env.local   S = .env.staging   P = .env.production      req = required   opt = optional

# VITE_ADMIN_API_BASE_URL   WHERE: L=opt (default http://localhost:5001)   S=req   P=req
# The address (origin, no path) of the ADMIN API. The site calls <this>/api/admin/v1. A final / is ignored.
# The API must allow THIS site's address through its ADMIN_ORIGINS (backend/.env.*), or the browser blocks every call. The admin site and the admin API must share one parent domain,
# so the staff login cookie is kept.
#   L, one API for everything (API_SURFACE=all, the default): http://localhost:5001
#   L, the two services run apart (backend\scripts\dev-service.ps1): http://localhost:5011 (the admin service)
#   S: https://admin-api.rootsquares.com (guide 41 Step 4)      P: the production admin API
VITE_ADMIN_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5001
